From 2ad836d0a3f0c358e5a72d45485d5330e032b96c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "togetherweown[bot]"
 <319968614+togetherweown[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 00:45:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] TOG-681: probe model health on the host scheduler, gate
 budget on measured spend
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three defects, one frame: this plugin must work on a stock host.

A dead-but-enabled cheap model was selected forever. Survivors sort
cheapest-first and the transport makes one attempt, so nothing ever
removed it. `enabled` was static because nothing could flip it —
`ctx.config` is read-only, so the fix cannot be a config mutation.

A manifest `jobs[]` entry now runs on the host's own plugin job
scheduler and reads each company's upstream catalogue, storing a
company-scoped health overlay that selection applies. The overlay only
ever removes a model from service: a model the operator disabled stays
disabled regardless of what the upstream says. Two consecutive absences
are required before disabling, and an indeterminate probe — transport
failure, 401, 503, non-JSON — changes nothing rather than blacking out
the whole table, which would be strictly worse than the defect. Flips go
to the board through activity.log.write.

The budget gates were inert: `budgetSpentFraction` was an optional the
caller supplied and nobody did, and `gateLevelFor` reads a non-finite
value as "ok". Completed calls with reported usage now accrue into a
monthly ledger and the fraction is computed from it. Unreported usage
counts as zero rather than an invented estimate, so the halt gate only
ever fires on numbers someone can audit. The caller-supplied signal is
gone — a caller that could set its own fraction could set it to zero and
walk through the halt gate.

Both are demonstrated end-to-end through the registered job handler and
the real action path, not only against the pure functions: a model
leaving the catalogue reroutes the next selection, and a ledger crossing
the halt line refuses non-pinned work while still honouring a pin.

check:host-coupling keeps the stock-host claim honest. It fails if a
host patch becomes reachable from anything that runs, if src imports a
private @paperclipai module or a path into a checkout, or if the packed
tarball starts carrying either. Each detector was mutation-tested.
decisions/0010 records why the router does not govern the model agents
run on, and the manifest's absent agents.* capability is what enforces
it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .github/workflows/ci.yml | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/.github/workflows/ci.yml b/.github/workflows/ci.yml
index 602093a..136bbd7 100644
--- a/.github/workflows/ci.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/ci.yml
@@ -42,6 +42,15 @@ jobs:
       - name: Assert the runtime entrypoints exist
         run: test -f dist/manifest.js && test -f dist/worker.js
 
+      # The README claims this plugin runs on a stock host — no checkout, no
+      # patch, no private host module. That claim is only as good as the last
+      # person who remembered it, so it is a gate: a host patch reachable from
+      # anything that runs, an /app path in src, or a private @paperclipai
+      # import fails the build. Runs before verify:host because it needs no
+      # host checkout and its failure explains the others.
+      - name: Assert no coupling to a modified host
+        run: npm run check:host-coupling
+
       # Validate the BUILT artifact with the host's own install-time validators
       # (`pluginManifestV1Schema` and the Ajv config validator), so a manifest
       # or config schema the host would reject fails here rather than at
